.\" essay.ms: the acceptance corpus's essay in the humanities
.\" (tests/host/latex_test.sh): one column, citations as the Chicago
.\" Manual's notes (biblatex-chicago's, from refer -S chicago's), an
.\" epigraph, a block quotation, notes of the author's own beside the
.\" citations, words in Greek, French, German and Latin, small capitals.
.columns 1
.title "The Return of the Virtues"
.subtitle "Aristotle, Anscombe and the Modern Moral Philosophers"
.author "Hélène Lecteur"
.affil "Faculté de Philosophie, Université de Paris, France"
.date "October 2026"
.abstract
In 1958 Elizabeth Anscombe told moral philosophers to stop doing moral
philosophy until they had a philosophy of psychology. This essay
traces what followed, from Philippa Foot to Alasdair MacIntyre, and
asks whether the virtues have returned or have only been named again.
.endabstract
.maketitle
.epigraph
For the things we have to learn before we can do them, we learn by
doing them.
.attrib "Aristotle, \fINicomachean Ethics\fP"
.endepigraph
.section "Anscombe's challenge" challenge

Anscombe's paper opens with three theses, the first of which is that
\(lqit is not profitable for us at present to do moral philosophy.\(rq
.cite anscombe1958 1
Her target was the moral \(lqought,\(rq which she thought survived from
a law conception of ethics after belief in a lawgiver had gone.\*[fnmark]
.fn
She credits the observation, in part, to Schopenhauer; see also
.cite macintyre1981 60-61
on the \(lqfailure of the Enlightenment project.\(rq
.fe
.p
The alternative she pointed to was older. Aristotle's word was
\fIἀρετή\fP, excellence, and the excellences of character are
acquired, as the epigraph says, by practice:
.quote
Neither by nature, then, nor contrary to nature do the excellences
arise in us; rather we are adapted by nature to receive them, and are
made perfect by habit.
.cite aristotle1999 1103a
.endquote
The French \fIhabitude\fP and the German \fIGewohnheit\fP lose the
sense of an achievement that the Greek \fIἕξις\fP keeps; the Latin
\fIhabitus\fP keeps it better. Even so, a state is not a feeling,
nor a capacity: \f(SCit is a settled disposition\fP.
.section "Foot and MacIntyre" foot

Philippa Foot answered first.
.cite foot1978 2-4
The virtues, she argued, are beneficial dispositions, \fIcorrective\fP
of temptations common to us all.\*[fnmark]
.fn
Courage corrects fear, temperance desire. The claim is Aristotle's
in outline,
.cite aristotle1999 1107a
though Foot did not say so.
.fe
MacIntyre went further, and made the virtues depend on practices,
traditions and the narrative unity of a life.
.cite macintyre1981 187
Martha Nussbaum's early work on Aristotle's psychology
.cite nussbaum1975
belongs to the same return, and so, more distantly, does the formal
logic of Łukasiewicz.
.cite lukasiewicz1957
.section "Conclusion" conclusion

The virtues have returned, in name at least. Whether Anscombe's
condition has been met \(em a philosophy of psychology adequate to
them \(em is another question.
.cite anscombe1958
.bibliography
